{{also|absúrd|absürd}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*swer- (swear)}}
First attested in 1557. From {{bor|en|frm|absurde}}, from {{der|en|la|absurdus||[[incongruous]], dissonant, out of tune}},<ref name=RHD>{{R:RHCD|page=7}}</ref> from {{m|la|ab||away from, out}} + {{m|la|surdus||silent, deaf, dull-sounding}}.<ref name=WI3>{{R:MW3 1976|page=8}}</ref> Compare {{m|en|surd}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/əbˈsɜːd/|/əbˈzɜːd/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/æbˈsɝd/|/æbˈzɝd/|/əbˈsɝd/|/əbˈzɝd/}}
* {{a|Canada}} {{IPA|en|/æbˈzɝd/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)d|s=2}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-absurd.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# Contrary to reason or [[propriety]]; [[obviously]] and [[flatly]] opposed to [[manifest]] truth; [[inconsistent]] with the plain [[dictates]] of common sense; [[logically]] [[contradictory]]; [[nonsensical]]; [[ridiculous]]; silly. {{defdate|from mid-16th c.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=10}}</ref>
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare 6-1|V|iv|text=This proffer is '''absurd''' and reasonless.}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Man|epistle=IV|text='Tis phrase '''absurd''' to call a villain great}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=17|passage=“Perhaps it is because I have been excommunicated. It's '''absurd''', but I feel like the Jackdaw of Rheims.”&nbsp;¶&nbsp;She winced and bowed her head. Each time that he spoke flippantly of the Church he caused her pain.}}
#* {{quote-song
|en
|lyricist=Roger Hodgson
|title=The Logical Song
|album=Breakfast in America
|artist=Supertramp
|year=1979
|passage=I know it sounds '''absurd''' / But please, tell me who I am}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[inharmonious|Inharmonious]]; [[dissonant]]. {{defdate|only early 17th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# Having no [[rational]] or [[orderly]] relationship to people's lives; [[meaningless]]; lacking order or value.
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=Adults have condemned them to live in what must seem like an '''absurd''' universe.|author=Joseph Featherstone|title=A New Kind of Schooling|work=The New Republic|date=March 2, 1968}}
# Dealing with [[absurdism]].

====Usage notes====
* In the comparative and superlative degrees, the forms ''more absurd'' and ''most absurd'' are usually preferred over {{m|en|absurder}}, {{m|en|absurdest}}.
* Webster 1913 has the sole definition "Contrary to reason or propriety; obviously and flatly opposed to manifest truth; inconsistent with the plain dictates of common sense; logically contradictory; nonsensical; ridiculous; as, an absurd person, an absurd opinion; an absurd dream."

